<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayeux Tapestry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1070s</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> Tapestry is an embroidered cloth about 70 metres long depicting the events leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman conquest of England</a>, ending with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066. It was probably made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the 1070s for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Odo of Bayeux and long hung in Bayeux Cathedral. Among its scenes is Count Guy of Ponthieu's capture of the shipwrecked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry.jpg" alt="Bayeux Tapestry" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odo_bayeux_tapestry.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
